    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#emperor-gaozu-of-tang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozu-of-tang.jpg" alt="Emperor Gaozu of Tang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#emperor-gaozu-of-tang">Emperor Gaozu of Tang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TangGaozu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Tang Dynasty was founded in 618 CE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emperor-gaozu-of-tang">Li Yuan</a> (Emperor Gaozu), Duke of Tang and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sui-dynasty">Sui</a> military commander at Taiyuan, who exploited the collapse of the Sui, brought down by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang</a>'s disastrous campaigns against the Korean kingdom of Goguryeo (the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#goguryeo-sui-war">Goguryeo–Sui War</a>) and the massive rebellions they provoked, to seize the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#changan">Chang'an</a> in 617 and proclaim a new dynasty the following year. The Tang founding was a family enterprise: Li Yuan's second son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a>, played the decisive military role in the campaigns that secured the dynasty, and in 626 staged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#xuanwu-gate-incident">Xuanwu Gate Incident</a>, an ambush in which he killed his brothers, the crown prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#li-jiancheng">Li Jiancheng</a> and Li Yuanji, before compelling his father to abdicate in his favour. As Emperor Taizong (r. 626–649), Li Shimin presided over what Chinese tradition celebrates as a golden age of humane government, military power and cultural creativity. His frontier policy was particularly striking: rather than simply excluding the steppe peoples, Taizong incorporated Turkic and other non-Han peoples into the Tang military and administrative system, and after the defeat of the Eastern Turks in 630 he was acclaimed '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#heavenly-khagan">Heavenly Khagan</a>' by their chieftains, a recognition that the Tang empire straddled the Chinese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#inner-asia">Inner Asian</a> political worlds. The interruption of the Tang line by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wu-zetian">Empress Wu Zetian</a> (r. 690–705), who proclaimed her own Zhou dynasty, was followed by the long reign of Emperor Xuanzong (r. 712–756), whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kaiyuan-era">Kaiyuan era</a> (713–741) is regarded by Chinese historians as the Tang's cultural and administrative zenith. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#census">census</a> of 754 the registered population stood at roughly 53 million, and Chang'an was among the largest and most cosmopolitan cities on earth.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#changan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/changan.jpg" alt="Chang'an" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#changan">Chang'an</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prince_Yide%27s_tomb,_towers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown Tang-era tomb artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At its peak the Tang economy was among the most sophisticated and commercially integrated in the world, sustained by an agricultural base organised under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#equal-field-system">equal-field</a> (juntian) system of land allocation and connected to transcontinental trade through the overland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk-road">Silk Road</a> and the maritime routes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-china-sea">South China Sea</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#changan">Chang'an</a>, with perhaps a million inhabitants within and around its walls, was a commercial centre of extraordinary cosmopolitanism: Sogdian, Persian, Arab, Indian, Korean, Japanese and Central Asian merchants traded in its two great markets, exchanging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">silk</a>, ceramics and Chinese manufactures for horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spice-trade">spices</a>, aromatics, glassware and luxury goods from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eurasia">Eurasia</a>. The equal-field system, which allocated land to adult males in return for tax and labour obligations, provided a relatively stable agricultural base in the early and middle Tang, though it broke down progressively as aristocratic and monastic estates absorbed land and reduced the state's ability to allocate holdings to new households. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#woodblock-printing">Woodblock printing</a> developed during the Tang and was used chiefly for Buddhist texts and calendars; the Diamond Sutra of 868, found at Dunhuang, is the oldest surviving dated printed book, though the commercial potential of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#printing-press">printing</a> would not be fully realised until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#song-dynasty">Song</a>. The catastrophic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#an-lushan-rebellion">An Lushan Rebellion</a> (755–763), in which the frontier general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#an-lushan">An Lushan</a> rose with his northeastern armies, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#luoyang">Luoyang</a> and Chang'an and forced Xuanzong to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sichuan">Sichuan</a>, completed the collapse of the equal-field system and devastated much of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>. In 780 the dynasty replaced the old per-capita levies with the two-tax system based on property, but it remained dependent on increasingly autonomous regional military governors (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jiedushi">jiedushi</a>) whose fiscal independence permanently reduced imperial revenues.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-talas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-talas.jpg" alt="Battle of Talas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-talas">Battle of Talas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Talas.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SY, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the early and middle Tang the dynasty's armies were among the most formidable in the world, combining the disciplined infantry and cavalry traditions of the Chinese agricultural core with the steppe cavalry skills of Turkic, Sogdian and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#inner-asia">Inner Asian</a> commanders and soldiers taken into imperial service. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fubing-system">fubing</a> militia system, inherited from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and refined by the early Tang, provided a force of farmer-soldiers whose service obligations were linked to land allocation, sparing the state the cost of a large permanent professional army. The early Tang campaigns were extraordinary in scope: the destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eastern-turkic-khaganate">Eastern Turkic Khaganate</a> in 630, the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gaochang">Gaochang</a> in 640, and under Taizong's successor Gaozong the defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a> in 657, which extended Tang suzerainty across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> and into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, bringing Chinese power to its greatest westward extent. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-talas">Battle of Talas</a> in 751, where a Tang army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gao">Gao</a> Xianzhi was defeated by Abbasid forces after the defection of its Karluk allies, is often cited as marking the limit of Tang westward expansion, and is traditionally linked with the transmission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#papermaking">paper-making</a> westward through captured craftsmen. By the 730s and 740s the fubing had decayed and been replaced on the frontiers by large professional armies under military governors. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#an-lushan-rebellion">An Lushan Rebellion</a> (755–763) showed that these frontier armies, professional, regionally rooted and loyal to their commanders, were both more powerful than any force the court could raise and politically uncontrollable. After 763 the dynasty depended increasingly on regional commanders whose loyalty could not be reliably compelled, and on palace armies controlled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eunuch">eunuchs</a>, and the balance between the imperial centre and regional power-holders never recovered the equilibrium of the early Tang.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#wei-zheng"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wei-zheng.jpg" alt="Wei Zheng" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#wei-zheng">Wei Zheng</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WeiZheng.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Taken by Fanghong, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Tang government was among the most sophisticated administrative systems of the medieval world, built on the structure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#three-departments-and-six-ministries">three departments and six ministries</a> (sansheng liubu) that became the template for Chinese imperial administration for centuries. The three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#departement">departments</a>, the Secretariat, the Chancellery and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#department-of-state-affairs">Department of State Affairs</a>, divided policy-making into successive stages of drafting, review and implementation, so that imperial edicts were subject to genuine institutional scrutiny before taking effect. The six ministries, Personnel, Revenue, Rites, War, Justice and Works, administered the functional domains of government through hierarchical bureaucracies whose officials were drawn from aristocratic families and, in growing numbers, from the civil service examinations, especially the prestigious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jinshi">jinshi</a> degree. Taizong's relationship with his ministers, particularly the remonstrating official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wei-zheng">Wei Zheng</a>, whose blunt criticisms the emperor famously accepted, became a model of sagely government that Chinese political thought idealised for centuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tang-code">Tang Code</a>, first issued in 624 and supplemented in 653 by an authoritative official commentary (Tang Lü Shuyi), was a comprehensive legal compilation that influenced the legal systems of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vietnam">Vietnam</a>, demonstrating the reach of Tang institutions across East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a>. The collapse of central authority after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#an-lushan-rebellion">An Lushan Rebellion</a> forced the dynasty to accept the entrenchment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jiedushi">jiedushi</a>, regional military governors who controlled taxation, appointments and armies in their provinces, while at court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eunuch">eunuchs</a> came to dominate the palace armies and the succession, as the failed attempt to purge them in the Sweet Dew Incident of 835 showed. This quasi-feudal decentralisation was never reversed and ultimately produced the Five Dynasties period that followed the dynasty's fall.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Tang Dynasty's historical impact operated through several channels at once: the commercial integration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eurasia">Eurasia</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk-road">Silk Road</a>, the cultural and institutional influence of Tang <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> on its East Asian neighbours, and technological developments, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#printing-press">printing</a>, refined ceramics and the earliest known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gunpowder">gunpowder</a> formulas recorded by Tang alchemists, that would eventually transform world history. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#changan">Chang'an</a>'s cosmopolitanism in the early and middle Tang had few equals anywhere: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#church-of-the-east">Nestorian Christians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a>, Manichaeans and Buddhists maintained temples and monasteries in the capital, alongside communities of Muslim merchants, while the state employed Sogdian financiers, Turkic generals, Korean officials and scholars from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#japan">Japan</a>. The Tang tributary order drew Japan, the Korean kingdoms and other neighbours into a hierarchical diplomatic framework of periodic missions to Chang'an, transmitting Chinese administrative models, writing, Buddhist institutions and material culture with consequences that long outlasted the dynasty. Japan's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#taika-reform">Taika Reform</a> (645) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#taiho-code">Taihō Code</a> (701) explicitly modelled themselves on Tang institutions, and Japanese embassies to the Tang carried monks and students who brought Tang learning home. Unified Silla absorbed Tang administrative law, Confucian learning and Buddhist culture. In the Red River delta, governed under the Tang as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#protectorate">Protectorate</a> of Annam from 679, Tang rule left deep institutional and cultural marks before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vietnam">Vietnam</a> secured its independence in 939. Ceramic technology reached a classical maturity under the Tang, with the white <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#xing-ware">Xing wares</a>, the three-colour (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sancai">sancai</a>) glazed wares and the painted wares of Changsha, which reached markets across the Islamic world and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-africa">East Africa</a> and established Chinese ceramics as a prestige commodity in world trade.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#an-lushan-rebellion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/an-lushan-rebellion.jpg" alt="An Lushan Rebellion" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#an-lushan-rebellion">An Lushan Rebellion</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:An_Lushan_Rebellion.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SY, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Tang Dynasty's decline followed a long arc of structural deterioration, punctuated by the catastrophe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#an-lushan-rebellion">An Lushan Rebellion</a> and ending in the warlordism of the late ninth century. The rebellion of 755–763 was the turning point: the registered population fell from about 53 million to under 17 million, a collapse that reflected both mass mortality and the breakdown of household registration, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#equal-field-system">equal-field</a> system disintegrated, the militarily autonomous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jiedushi">jiedushi</a> rose to power and central fiscal authority was permanently weakened. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#huang-chao-rebellion">Huang Chao Rebellion</a> (874–884), a vast uprising led by a former salt smuggler, which sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 880 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#changan">Chang'an</a> in 881, was the terminal blow: it destroyed much of what remained of the institutional fabric of central government, killed great numbers of the educated elite who staffed the bureaucracy, and transferred effective power to warlords who used the crisis to consolidate regional dominance. The last Tang emperors were puppets of competing warlords, and the dynasty's formal end came in 907 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zhu-wen">Zhu Wen</a>, a former lieutenant of Huang Chao who had defected to the Tang and risen to dominate the court, deposed the last emperor and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#later-liang">Later Liang</a>. The ensuing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#five-dynasties-and-ten-kingdoms-period">Five Dynasties and Ten Kingdoms period</a> (907–960) was a prolonged political fragmentation, yet it preserved much of the Tang institutional and cultural inheritance and fostered the commercial and agricultural developments that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#song-dynasty">Song</a> would systematise. The Tang legacy, its administrative templates, legal codes, literary culture, Buddhist institutions and commercial networks, was so thoroughly embedded in East Asian civilisation that later Chinese dynasties defined themselves as its heirs, and its influence on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vietnam">Vietnam</a> endured long after the dynasty itself had fallen.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Tang Dynasty represents the apex of Chinese imperial cosmopolitanism, the moment when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#changan">Chang'an</a> stood at the centre of a vast Eurasian world, drawing talent, commerce and culture from across the continent and radiating Chinese institutional and cultural models across East and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> with consequences that persist to the present day.</em></p>

